{"sounds":{"intro":"This is Old Norse of the Viking Age, as written down in 13th-century Iceland (the sagas). The spelling used here is the normalised one that scholars use. Nobody speaks it, but Icelandic is its closest living relative. The pronunciation is a scholars' reconstruction.","rows":[["ð","like \"th\" in \"this\"","faðir"],["þ","like \"th\" in \"thing\"","þing"],["á / é / í / ó / ú / ý","the same vowels, held long","hús"],["ǫ","like \"o\" in \"more\", but open and short","ǫl"],["ø / œ","like German \"ö\"","øxi"],["æ","like \"a\" in \"cat\"","ær (before)"],["j","like \"y\" in \"yes\"","jǫrð"],["r","rolled","ráð"]],"notes":["Nouns have three genders, four cases and singular and plural. Many masculine nouns end in -r in the nominative: dagr, hestr, maðr.","This pool is much smaller than the Spanish one on purpose. Only well-attested words and forms are included. The pronunciation is reconstructed and nothing has been checked by a specialist, so treat it as a first guide."]},"numbers":[{"id":"1-to-10","title":"1 to 10","items":[["one","einn","ayn","einn"],["two","tveir","TVAYR","tveir"],["three","þrír","THREER","þrír"],["four","fjórir","FYOH-rir","fjórir"],["five","fimm","fimm","fimm"],["six","sex","seks","sex"],["seven","sjau","SYOW","sjau"],["eight","átta","AHT-tah","átta"],["nine","níu","NEE-oo","níu"],["ten","tíu","TEE-oo","tíu"]]},{"id":"bigger-numbers","title":"Bigger numbers","items":[["twenty","tuttugu","TOOT-too-goo","tuttugu"],["one hundred","hundrað","HOON-drath","hundrað"],["one thousand","þúsund","THOO-soond","þúsund"]]}],"greetings":[{"id":"hello-and-goodbye","title":"Hello and goodbye","items":[["hail (to a man)","heill","hayl","heill"],["hail (to a woman)","heil","hayl","heil"],["good day","góðan dag","GOH-than dahg","góðan dag"],["farewell","far vel","fahr vel","far vel"],["be well","vertu heill","VER-too hayl","vertu heill"]]},{"id":"meeting","title":"Meeting","items":[["how are you?","hvernig ferr þér","HVER-nig fehr thyehr","hvernig ferr þ